## 1. The reframe

**Airports are loud because nobody in the building trusts anyone else. Specify trust; the quiet follows.**

Every announcement in an airport terminal is compensation for a missing user experience. Every gate-swarm is compensation for a FIDS that isn't believed. Every gate agent shouting a group number over the din is compensation for a boarding process that doesn't self-organize. Every "final boarding call, Mr. Smith" is compensation for a check-in system that could not tell the airline where Mr. Smith actually is inside the terminal. Every muted-TV overlay of a chyron is compensation for a wayfinding system that gave up trying to hold your attention. The decibel level in a US airport terminal is not a design failure of the acoustic package. It is the *symptom* of a design failure in the information contract between the airport, the airlines, the tenants, and the passenger.

So the mainstream framing of a "quiet-by-design" standard — pick the right NC target, tune the RT60, tighten the STI, add PA zoning, spec directional speakers — is doing the second-order work. The first-order work is deciding what the terminal is *telling* each of its four users at each point in the journey, and then removing the noise that only exists because those messages aren't landing. Fix the trust problem and half the acoustic problem disappears without a dB meter ever coming out of the case. Miss the trust problem and you can spend $9 billion on absorbers and you'll still hear the gate agent yelling "GROUP FOUR" in Concourse C.

The one-line version, for the Strategist to steal: **A quiet terminal is a terminal where nobody has an operational reason to make noise. Everything else is treating the symptom.**

