## 2. Five connect-the-dots

### (a) The hospital alarm-fatigue playbook is the actual template — not Schiphol

Everyone will cite Schiphol, Helsinki, and London City. Fine, cite them. But the useful analog is not another airport; it's the hospital ward. US hospitals spent a decade discovering that their monitored units were dangerously loud, that clinical staff were tuning out alarms, and that "reduce alarms" as a policy did nothing. What worked was tiered redesign: change the threshold on the pulse-ox alarm from 90% to 88% and Johns Hopkins knocked out 63% of alarms in a coronary care unit without changing a single policy about *whether* to alarm. Boston Medical Center went from a million alarms a week to 400,000 by eliminating the low-priority ones entirely.[^1] [^2] The analog is exact: the reason MWAA terminals sound the way they sound is not that gate agents love the microphone. It's that the announcement threshold has drifted to "every named passenger, every irregular event, every airline preference," and the operator on the floor has no permission to say no. **A quiet-by-design standard is an alarm-tiering standard first and an acoustic standard second.** Write the tiering and the acoustic targets become achievable. Write the acoustic targets first and the tiering never happens.

The one-line version: **PA overuse is alarm fatigue in a bigger room, and airports lag hospitals by about fifteen years in noticing.**

### (b) The Saarinen problem: MWAA already has a quiet-by-design terminal from 1962, and we ruined it

Dulles Main Terminal was designed as an acoustic cathedral. It has the volume, the reflective glass, the soaring section, and the deliberate ceremonial hush of a mid-century civic room. Grand Central's Whispering Gallery ended up an acoustic accident of Guastavino tiling — sound waves that had nowhere absorbent to go.[^3] The Main Terminal is the same phenomenon in a larger key. And it is now one of the loudest spaces in the MWAA system, because for sixty-four years we have layered mobile-lounge scrums, hard-surface CBP screening lines, PA loudspeakers designed for coverage instead of intelligibility, muted TVs with captioned news, retail with its own overhead audio, and a wayfinding system that gave up and delegated to the human voice. The lesson generalizes to Concourse E and to whatever replaces C and D: the acoustic performance of a new terminal is not set by the shell design. It is set by *what we allow to be added on top of the shell after the ribbon is cut*. **The tenant design manual is the acoustic standard.** If MWAA's design manual specifies NC-35 in a hold room and the concessionaire specifies retail-standard NC-45 in the store next door, the concessionaire wins by physics.

The one-line version: **Airports are quiet on drawing day and loud on opening day, because the drawings don't govern the retail fitouts and the audio-visual scope.**

### (c) DCA's slot economics set a *higher* floor on announcement density than IAD's — and any common standard that ignores this will destroy value

This is the airline-side asymmetry nobody in a quiet-airport conversation ever raises. At IAD, a missed international boarding is a rebooking cost, a bag-pull, a schedule irritant. At DCA, a missed boarding on a slot-controlled flight is not just a rebooking cost; the slot itself has enterprise value, and a chronically late departure erodes the on-time performance profile that airlines optimize their entire DCA operation around. DCA is one of the most fought-over slot pools in the country — the FAA cap of 67 hourly slots and the perimeter rule mean the marginal minute of gate-hold has a different unit economics than at IAD.[^4] [^5] The cost of a passenger who wandered off to Legal Sea Foods and missed the boarding is asymmetric at DCA. That means DCA's *rational* announcement density is higher than IAD's. A common MWAA standard that treats "fewer announcements" as universally good will optimize one airport and quietly transfer cost to the other. **Common standard on the acoustic envelope and the intelligibility floor; different standards on announcement policy and quiet-zone geometry, because the underlying airline economics are different.** The equivalent in another domain: financial exchanges kept the open-outcry pit loud long after everyone else went digital, because the marginal cost of a missed trade justified the acoustic externality. DCA is closer to a trading floor than to Helsinki.

The one-line version: **DCA is a slot-controlled airport. That is an acoustic fact before it is a scheduling fact.**

### (d) CNN Airport Network died in 2021 because nobody could tell it not to — and the loudest thing coming back onto our walls right now is its replacement

CNN pulled the Airport Network out of 58 airports on March 31, 2021, and cited streaming and COVID-era traffic as the reason.[^6] The actual story was messier: passengers hated it, contract-required volume ranges made it unmutable, and airports had signed away the acoustic environment of their own hold rooms to a media company for a slice of revenue. The moment CNN dropped it, the wall real estate did not go dark; it filled with a next-generation product from a different vendor, this time streaming, sometimes silent, sometimes not.[^7] The lesson is not "kill the TVs." It is that the acoustic budget of a US terminal is a *contractual* variable, negotiated one concession lease at a time, and nobody in the acoustic conversation is ever in the concession-lease conversation. **If the standard doesn't touch tenant AV rights, the standard doesn't touch the noise.** MWAA's Vol. 2 and Vol. 3 tenant design standards, and the DCA parallel volumes, are where the fight is actually won or lost. Not in the terminal package.

The one-line version: **The single loudest thing on the wall in most US airports has never been the PA. It's whatever we sold the wall to.**

### (e) The silent-airport model is a blue-sky policy. The real spec is the *surge*

Helsinki went "Silent Airport" in June 2015. London City did the same in 2008. Schiphol has run a variant since roughly 2011.[^8] [^9] [^10] The public claim is that this works. The operator-honest claim is that this works *when the schedule is running*. The minute you have IRROPS — snow, an FAA ground stop, a helicopter-vs-CRJ incident on approach like DCA experienced in January 2025 — the silent-airport model collapses. Passengers don't have flights to look up. FIDS is telling half-truths. Rebooking lines form and staff need to shout. This is the emergency-management pattern that every EOC director will recognize: the base state and the surge state are two different specifications, and if you design the base state without specifying the surge, the surge is what breaks. Hospital surge capacity works the same way — quiet unit protocols suspend during a code, and everyone knows the ruleset flips. **The MWAA quiet-by-design standard should be written in two states: a normal-ops spec that governs 95% of hours, and an IRROPS spec that specifies what "graceful loudness" looks like when the airport is under stress.** If we don't spec the surge, we'll spec a policy the operator can't defend on a bad Tuesday, and it'll be ignored on the first snowstorm.

The one-line version: **The silent-airport policy is a good-weather policy. The interesting question is what the standard says on the worst day.**

