## 3. The operator's gut check

The line I would actually say at the bar, verbatim, for the Strategist to lift:

> "Look — if we actually build a quiet terminal, here's what we'll hear that we don't hear now: every roll-aboard on the terrazzo, the guy on the phone yelling at his kid, the flight attendant on speakerphone with crew scheduling, the busker with the amp at Terminal 1. Quiet doesn't mean silent. Quiet means the *human* noise stops being hidden by the *system* noise. That's the point. Nobody planning this is thinking about it that way, and the first complaint letter after opening is going to be from somebody who says the terminal used to be more peaceful."

The other thing an experienced ops leader would say: nobody who has run an EOC believes you can specify the ambient environment of a hold room in isolation from the airline gate-agent culture at that airport. United gate agents in Concourse C use the PA differently than American gate agents in DCA Terminal 2. Those cultures are the acoustic environment. A standard that doesn't have an airline-facing chapter — signed onto by United at IAD and American at DCA, not just referenced in the design manual — is a standard that will be ignored by the people making 80% of the noise.

And the third thing, the one where my operator gut overrules the published wisdom: **the accessibility argument for redundant channels is often deployed as a reason to keep more announcements, and it is being used to protect a status quo that actively excludes the same populations it claims to protect.** A hold room with a constantly-active PA is worse for a passenger with sensory processing differences, worse for a hearing-aid user chasing garbled speech in reverb, worse for a passenger with limited English who parses a chyron better than a scratchy Announcement. The right redundancy is *silent visual*, *personal digital*, *staffed human contact*, *and* short, high-intelligibility spoken alerts — in that order, not the reverse. Anyone who tells you "we can't reduce announcements because of ADA" has the argument exactly backwards.

